{"doe":{"id":"802573cb-e274-4722-92c2-6cab0ebfce53","word":"doe","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"female deer","tags":["feminine"],"word":"corça"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"female rabbit","tags":["feminine"],"word":"coelha"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"female hare","tags":["feminine"],"word":"lebre"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"A doe from round a spruce stood looking at them\nAcross the wall, as near the wall as they.\nShe saw them in their field, they her in hers.","ref":"1923 October, Robert Frost, “[Grace Notes.] Two Look at Two.”, in New Hampshire […], New York, N.Y.: Henry Holt and Company, →OCLC, page 95:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[2,5]],"id":"6acc2e11-4327-4165-8a60-e8e71748e274"},{"text":"The city recently carried out a deer census, determining there are 313 stags (males), 798 does (females) and 214 fawns (babies) in Nara Park.","ref":"2024 July 23, Lilit Marcus, “Japan may be sick of mass tourism. But the deer in this ancient UNESCO-listed city love it”, in CNN:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[90,94]],"id":"a2dc48d7-b21a-4f37-b98b-c59eb64e301f"}],"links":[["deer","deer"],["antelope","antelope"],["goat","goat"],["nanny","nanny#English"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A female deer; also used of similar animals such as antelope (less commonly a goat, as nanny is also used)."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"7a765371-7f6e-41b6-a02e-2665bb02f61e"},{"links":[["female","female"],["fallow deer","fallow deer"]],"raw_glosses":["(specifically) A female fallow deer."],"glosses":["A female fallow deer."],"tags":["countable","specifically","uncountable"],"id":"bca2cfd7-3512-4fd7-bac2-6b4eb7eeafdf"},{"links":[["rabbit","rabbit"],["hare","hare"]],"glosses":["A female rabbit or hare."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"83daa47e-8922-4369-bb32-4df1f8602d79"},{"links":[["squirrel","squirrel"]],"glosses":["A female squirrel."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"e4d40791-1756-43f8-867a-6ad59b576668"},{"links":[["kangaroo","kangaroo"]],"glosses":["A female kangaroo."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"66f8ee16-f18c-4d6b-a697-d56df3b59968"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"sense":"female deer","english":"female red deer","translation":"female red deer","word":"hind"},{"sense":"female kangaroo","english":"female red kangaroo","translation":"female red kangaroo","word":"blue flyer"}]},"doer":{"id":"0c28f0c5-aa18-4642-9bc3-6a4bda612069","word":"doer","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"someone who does","tags":["masculine"],"word":"fazedor"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"someone who does","tags":["feminine"],"word":"fazedora"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"a doer of good","bold_text_offsets":[[2,6]],"type":"example","id":"65daa40c-6746-41f3-9da9-d9fae5e5720e"},{"text":"By the wisdom proclaimed of old the doer must suffer.","ref":"1924, Herbert Weir Smyth, “VIII. The Orestea: III. Eumenides”, in Aeschylean Tragedy, page 205:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[36,40]],"id":"44a2096c-262a-4be9-818b-2029ec86992d"},{"text":"The doer and the thinker\nNo allowance for the other.","ref":"1972, “Thick As A Brick”, Ian Anderson (lyrics), performed by Jethro Tull:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,8]],"id":"20d3c2f6-f645-43e5-a1a9-b04b5c493d21"},{"text":"Though his name was closely linked to that of Physiocrats, he was less an armchair intellectual like Quesnay or the elder Mirabeau than a doer in the vein of Bertin and Trudaine [...].","ref":"2002, Colin Jones, The Great Nation, Penguin, published 2003, page 295:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[138,142]],"id":"6b5e1194-afb5-4695-a9fc-106b35875aee"},{"text":"In schools, submission, not curiosity, was a highly valued virtue. Thinkers were out, doers were in.","ref":"2008 March 25, Aleksa